186163073 #edb/ref 1061 38047 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163073 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a66882b2-817d-57e1-87b6-b618cba85d65" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163073 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162109 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163074 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163075 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163074 #edb/ref 1061 38048 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163074 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "384b68eb-0905-5ee4-9ed9-92926dca6994" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163074 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162110 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163075 #edb/ref 1061 38051 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163075 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "65571357-9862-53b7-a78a-da750210d350" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163075 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162111 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163327 #edb/ref 1064 "Let $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ be a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a normal population $N(\\mu,\\sigma^2)$ with mean $\\mu$ and variance $\\sigma^2.$ In other words,   \n\n$$\nX_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n} \\sim N(\\mu,\\sigma^2).\n$$\n\nIn this case, we can easily determine the sampling distribution of the sample mean\n\n$$\n\\overline{X} = \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n X_i.\n$$\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- The sum of $n$ independent and identically distributed normal random variables is normally distributed. Thus, \n\n$$\nY = \\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n X_i\n$$\n\nis normally distributed.\n\n- A normally distributed random variable scaled by a constant factor is normally distributed. Thus, \n\n$$\n\\overline{X} = \\dfrac1n\\cdot Y\n$$\n\nis normally distributed.\n\nAlso, we previously saw that the expected value of the sample mean equals the population mean, i.e., \n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\overline{X}] = \\mu,\n$$\n\nand the variance of the sample mean is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[\\overline{X}] = \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n}.\n$$\n\nPutting all of this together, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\overline{X} \\sim N\\left(\\mu, \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n}\\right),\n$$\n\nIn other words, the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ is normally distributed with mean $\\mu$ and variance $\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163327 #edb/ref 1065 6277 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163327 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "14d0c44e-7b46-50e5-800f-e73c03b66e46" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163327 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186428856 #edb/ref 1030 1.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428856 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account multivariable: 1 rises / 1 eligible passed source activities = 1.000000; nearest selected fraction = 1. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: multivariable=1 (1/1). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: False. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/multivariable-frequency-estimates.json, pair 3067->2980. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428856 #edb/ref 1036 #edb/ref 17592186050606 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428857 #edb/ref 1030 1.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428857 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account multivariable: 1 rises / 1 eligible passed source activities = 1.000000; nearest selected fraction = 1. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: multivariable=1 (1/1). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: False. Crossing frequency is the 